    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-111578": {
      "title": "H.C. Wang Assistant Professor in Mathematics",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities H.C. Wang Assistant Professor in Mathematics H.C. Wang Assistant Professor in Mathematics The Mathematics Department at Cornell University invites applications for two H.C. Wang Assistant Professors, non-tenure track, non-renewable, 3-year positions beginning July 1, 2027. Successful candidates are expected to pursue independent research at Cornell and teach three courses per year. A Ph.D. in mathematics is required. Applicants must apply electronically by submitting the following materials at https://www.mathjobs.org : Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching statement Research statement Four reference letters, including one that addresses the candidate's teaching experience Deadline is December 1, 2026. Disclosure: Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointments to academic positions require candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct . Pay Range: salary will be at least $85,900 Actual salary offers in the College of Arts and Sciences will be based on education, experience, discipline, and relevant skills. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: https://www.mathjobs.org/jobs/list/29164 Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Teaching statement Four reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://www.math.cornell.edu/ Sam Loew chair_asst_math@cornell.edu Cornell University Mathematics Department 310 Malott Hall Ithaca, NY 14853-4201 Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-09 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-111573": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Postdoctoral Associate Postdoctoral Associate Academic Job Description Department of Global Development Postdoctoral Associate The College of Agriculture and Life Sciences (CALS) is a pioneer of purpose-driven science and home to Cornell University’s second largest population of students, faculty and staff. We work across disciplines to tackle the challenges of our time through world-renowned research, education and outreach. The questions we probe and the answers we seek focus on three overlapping concerns: natural and human systems; food, energy and environmental resources; and social, physical and economic well-being. CALS is fundamentally invested in improving the lives of people, their environments and their communities both in New York state and around the world. Cornell's Food Systems & Global Change (FSGC) team is a part of the Department of Global Development, College of Agricultural and Life Sciences and focuses on increasing the sustainability of food systems for the benefit of humans and ecosystems. We work in the areas of sustainable intensification of agriculture, climate mitigation and adaptation, livestock systems, and healthy and sustainable diets, providing analysis and insights aimed at helping to shape the decision-making process at all relevant levels. The livestock sector is a crucial component of the food system and, due to its impacts and contributions, can play a defining role in any sensible development strategy. We are looking for a motivated postdoc to provide support in performing analyses to guide decision making in the livestock sector in order to support sustainable food systems transitions. Position Function Livestock systems are important for food security, rural livelihoods, land use, nutrient cycling, and climate change mitigation and adaptation. However, they can also contribute to greenhouse gas emissions, pressure on land and water resources, and other environmental impacts. Identifying effective interventions therefore requires a systems perspective that brings together animal biology, herd and flock management, feed resources, land use, environmental outcomes, economic feasibility, and adoption potential across diverse socioeconomic contexts. We are seeking a Postdoctoral Associate to support research on livestock systems and sustainable food-system transitions. The successful candidate will contribute to multi-scale and multidimensional analyses designed to guide mitigation and adaptation interventions in the livestock sector, including changes in herd and flock management, animal nutrition, feed resources, animal genetics, land use, grazing management, and emerging technologies. The position will involve working with large regional and global datasets, developing and applying quantitative models, evaluating environmental and productivity outcomes, and supporting the design and analysis of case studies. The successful candidate will work as part of an interdisciplinary team and collaborate with researchers, students, external partners, and stakeholders. The position will involve data analysis, model development, manuscript preparation, project coordination, occasional mentoring of junior researchers, and dissemination of findings through peer-reviewed publications, reports, presentations, and stakeholder-facing outputs. Responsibilities The Postdoctoral Associate will be expected to: Evaluate mitigation and adaptation strategies ranging from incremental management changes to more transformative interventions, including changes in diets, grazing management, feed resources, animal genetics, plant breeding, land use, and emerging technologies. Conduct multi-scale analyses of livestock production systems, including global, regional, national, and case-study applications. Characterize livestock systems across ruminant and monogastric species, including herd and flock parameters and dynamics, feed composition, and variables related to production-system typologies. Develop, adapt, and apply quantitative models to assess livestock interventions, including process-based, biophysical, spatial, and integrated assessment approaches. Integrate biophysical, environmental, economic, and social dimensions to assess intervention feasibility, tradeoffs, adoption potential, and context-specific outcomes Analyze greenhouse gas emissions and other environmental impacts associated with livestock production systems, including methane, nitrous oxide, feed-related emissions, land-use interactions, and nutrient flows. Support the identification and development of case studies for livestock management innovations and define typologies for interventions in livestock systems. Work with large spatial and tabular datasets, including regional and global livestock, land-use, feed, climate, environmental, and sustainability indicator datasets. Contribute to the development and documentation of livestock feed libraries, sustainability indicators, modelling workflows, and reproducible data pipelines. Collaborate with internal and external research partners, including interdisciplinary teams working across livestock, food systems, climate, land use, economics, and policy. Prepare peer-reviewed manuscripts, technical reports, policy-relevant outputs, and presentations for academic and professional audiences. Present research findings at conferences, seminars, project meetings, and stakeholder workshops. Mentor or provide technical guidance to graduate students, undergraduate students, visiting scholars, or junior researchers, as appropriate. Support project coordination, data management, reporting, and other organizational activities associated with collaborative research projects. This is an in-person position. The successful candidate will be expected to relocate within commuting distance of Ithaca, NY. Anticipated Division of Time Research, modelling, data analysis, and manuscript preparation – 80% Collaboration, project coordination, reporting, and stakeholder engagement – 10% Mentoring and technical support to students or junior researchers – 10% Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Animal Science, Environmental Science, Agricultural Sciences, Agronomy, Soil Science, Ecology, Geography, Agricultural Engineering, Global Development, or a closely related field. Demonstrated experience in livestock systems research, livestock systems modelling, environmental assessment, or food-system sustainability analysis. Strong understanding of livestock production systems, including ruminant and/or monogastric systems, herd or flock dynamics, productivity, feed use, and management practices. Demonstrated quantitative modelling skills, including experience with process-based models, biophysical models, spatial models, integrated assessment models, or related approaches. Capacity to analyze how livestock systems interact with soils, plants, climate, and other components of agricultural ecosystems, and assess the resulting synergies and trade-offs across food systems domains. Familiarity with methods for estimating greenhouse gas emissions and environmental impacts from livestock systems. Experience working with large datasets and reproducible analytical workflows. Proficiency in R, Python, or another relevant programming language. Ability to handle, analyze, and interpret spatial data. Strong analytical skills and excellent written and oral communication abilities. Track record of leading peer-reviewed publications or demonstrated ability to contribute substantially to high-quality, publishable research. Proven effectiveness to work independently while contributing effectively to interdisciplinary and collaborative research teams. Preferred Qualifications Experience developing or applying livestock feed libraries, animal nutrition models, whole-farm models, or soil–plant–animal models at different spatial scales. Familiarity with adoption analysis, stakeholder preferences, qualitative or mixed-methods research, or participatory modelling approaches. Experience working with global livestock, land-use, climate, remote-sensing. Satellite images, or sustainability indicator datasets. Competence with GIS tools and spatial analysis software. Experience with uncertainty analysis, scenario analysis, sensitivity analysis, or model comparison. Interest in using artificial intelligence tools to improve literature synthesis, coding workflows, data processing, model development, or scientific communication, while maintaining scientific rigor and reproducibility. Demonstrated ability to communicate complex technical findings to scientific, policy, and applied audiences. Proven success mentoring undergraduate or graduate students. Supervision Exercised There are no supervisory responsibilities for this position. To apply: Please apply via Academic Jobs Online ( https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32912 ) . Qualified candidates should submit- A cover letter describing the candidate’s interest in the position, goals for the postdoctoral appointment, alignment of the candidate’s expertise with the position requirements, and anticipated start date. A CV, including a publication list, date of Ph.D. graduation, and the name of the Ph.D. Advisor. A research statement of no more than 1,000 words describing the candidate’s research experience, main research interests and contributions, and future research directions, including how these relate to the proposed postdoctoral work. Names and contact information for three references, who will be contacted after the initial screening. A writing sample consisting of a peer-reviewed journal publication authored by the candidate. Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointments to academic positions require candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. Applications will be reviewed as received, continuing until a suitable applicant is identified. CALS hiring rate for this title is $63,480 - $70,000 College of Agriculture and Life Sciences Life. Changing. Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League and Land-grant university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students, and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery, and engagement. Cornell’s regional and global presence includes state-wide Cornell Cooperative Extension programs and offices in all counties and boroughs, global partnerships with institutions and communities engaged in life-changing research and education, the medical college’s campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, and the Cornell Tech campus on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. With a founding principle of “any person, any study,” Cornell is an equal opportunity employer . Pay Range: $62,232.00 - $88,745.00 Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-02 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-111564": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Assistant Teaching Professor Assistant Teaching Professor The Department of Biomedical and Translational Sciences (BTS) in the College of Veterinary Medicine (CVM) at Cornell University in Ithaca, NY is seeking an exceptional non-tenure track faculty member (teaching professor at the assistant level) whose area of teaching expertise is in reproductive physiology. The CVM at Cornell is a top-ranked veterinary college and houses a world-renowned Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM) curriculum. The BTS department within the CVM is a widely recognized unit with long-standing educational and research & discovery missions. On the educational side, BTS is deeply committed to excellence in teaching and mentoring at the pre-college, undergraduate, graduate, and professional (DVM) levels. This position will be 90% teaching and 10% academic service (no clinical service). The successful candidate will be joining a rich community of outstanding and dedicated teaching professors in the department and across the entire college, with a commitment to educational excellence and pedagogical innovation. Required qualifications: PhD or DVM or equivalent degree in a relevant discipline Evidence of past research experience and teaching dedication and excellence in animal science, especially reproductive biology Experience in and/or penchant for collaborative, team-based teaching Interest and willingness to co-lead a newly established Master’s program in reproductive medicine Interest and willingness to participate in problem-based learning (PBL) veterinary courses and traditional lecture-based courses Commitment to collegiality, intellectual humility, and strong communication and interpersonal skills with students, staff, and faculty If associate professor-level or above, dedication to training and mentorship of early-career teachers The teaching effort will be divided evenly between Foundation Course III (FC3) in the veterinary curriculum and the newly established Master’s program in Fundamental and Translational Reproductive Medicine (MS-FTRM). The primary focus of FC3 (Function & Dysfunction) is small and large animal veterinary physiology, pathophysiology, and pharmacology. The successful candidate will be expected to: Serve on the course design group (CDG), to help the course leader develop, revise, and run a highly effective course that serves the students’ needs and fulfills the educational mission. This includes participating in regular meetings throughout the year, and accomplishing tasks as assigned by the course leader across a wide range of course related issues, including course content, structure, assessments, student issues, grading, case study updates, etc. Facilitate small group sessions (referred to as “tutor groups”) and give lectures in both FC3a (Spring semester) and FC3b (Fall semester), as well as develop and supervise laboratory sessions. The most critical lecture and lab needs are in reproductive physiology, but the successful candidate will be expected to help with any topical areas as determined by the FC3 course leader. For the MS-FTRM program, the successful candidate will be expected to: Provide instruction and/or instructional support for the MS-FTRM foundation courses, including Comparative Molecular Strategies of Reproduction, Advanced Techniques in Reproduction, and Emerging Topics in Reproductive Medicine. This includes giving lectures, managing lab protocols, preparing lab equipment and supplies, helping develop grading rubrics, and even leading instruction some weeks. •Serve on the MS-FTRM leadership team and help organize monthly meetings of the leadership team. Organize and attend regular meetings of the MS-FTRM oversight committee. Participate in annual admissions process, including review of applications, virtual interviews, and communication with prospective students. Coordinate communication with external partners, particularly those who are providing slots for externships. •Advise students in the program, including support for classroom learning, matching of research interests with externship opportunities, and guidance on career development and employment options. Establish and regularly update the field handbook for the Graduate Field of Reproductive Medicine. Work with the college Marketing & Communications team to update information on the MS-FTRM website and promote advertising of the program. Coordinate ongoing evaluation of the MS-FTRM program from participating students, faculty, staff, and alumni in order to continuously improve the program. Interested candidates should submit the following: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Statement of teaching experiences and philosophy (2 pages maximum) Contact information for three professional references Electronic application submission is through Academic Jobs online: https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32995 Applications will be accepted immediately, and committee review will commence on October 23. For additional information, visit BTS, or contact Dr. Praveen Sethupathy, Chair of the Search Committee. The salary range for this full-time 12 month/year, non-tenure-track faculty appointment is $90,000-125,000. Offers higher than the published salary range might be considered to meet unique circumstances, qualifications, academic discipline, and experience. Faculty working in the laboratory must receive the pre-exposure rabies prophylaxis vaccination series prior to or immediately after hire or provide documentation of current vaccination. Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointments to academic positions require candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. Pay Range: $26.28 - $30.54 Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-09 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-111539": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Assistant Professor - Chemistry for Sustainable/Healthy Foods Assistant Professor - Chemistry for Sustainable/Healthy Foods Faculty Position Available: Assistant Professor Chemistry for Sustainable/Healthy Foods Department of Food Science, Cornell University Position: Assistant Professor, Tenure-track Location: Cornell University, Ithaca, NY 14853-5701, USA. The academic home for this position is the Department of Food Science in the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences. The Department of Food Science at Cornell University invites candidates for a 9-month, full-time, tenure-track assistant professor position to develop a research and teaching program related to Food Chemistry. While the department has identified several strategic areas of interest, listed below, exceptional candidates in Food Chemistry whose expertise lies outside the listed priority areas are also encouraged to apply. Chemistry for Sustainable Foods: Appropriate research areas may include: Chemistry of sustainably sourced food components (flavor, color, nutritive and bioactive compounds) that promote human or animal health while ensuring a palatable and resilient food supply; Strategies to extend shelf-life, reduce food waste, and enhance nutritional quality through mechanistic understanding of chemical changes during processing and storage. Foods for Health: Appropriate research areas may include: Food for health approaches, including mechanistic studies on how food components and bioactives influence physiological function, gut health, and the microbiome; Development and application of state-of-the-art physicochemical and analytical tools for characterization of novel and functional food components; Computational and systems-level approaches to understand key chemical and biochemical transformations in foods. Chemical Food Safety and Food Toxicology: Appropriate research areas may include: Development and utilization of rapid, inexpensive, and/or in situ sensors for real-time detection of chemical hazards (e.g., allergens, heavy metals, mycotoxins, and emerging contaminants) across the food system; Food toxicology, including mechanistic and translational studies of dietary exposure to chemical hazards and their impacts on human and animal health; Risk assessment, predictive modeling, sampling strategies, and exposure assessment for chemical contaminants in foods; Novel analytical and/or computational approaches to detect adulteration of raw materials or finished products; Strategies for mitigation, detoxification, or prevention of chemical hazards in food systems. Position Responsibilities: This position has a balanced effort between research (50%) and teaching (50%) on a 9-month academic year basis. A track-record of outstanding peer-reviewed scholarship is expected, as is excellence in and commitment to teaching, translation of knowledge, and advising and mentoring of students. We seek a colleague with an outstanding record demonstrating success and promise across all these areas, and who will be supported by and contribute to a vibrant culture of inclusive excellence at Cornell. A mentoring program for new faculty will provide guidance and assistance. Research (50%): The successful candidate will be expected to conduct an internationally recognized and externally funded research program in chemistry for sustainable/healthy foods. The applicant need not be from a food science background but should be willing to develop a program centered around food. Teaching (50%): The candidate will be expected to teach one undergraduate course per semester, and a third course every other year that could be a graduate seminar, or a course of equivalent effort. One undergraduate course is expected to be a general education course related to food science, and one is expected to be an upper-level food chemistry course. Specific courses taught may be adjusted based on departmental needs. The incumbent will be expected to advise undergraduate students and mentor Master’s and Ph.D. students. Service to the Department: The candidate will be expected to work collaboratively with the food science community, participate in faculty meetings, select committees, and related service work appropriate to faculty members. Qualifications: A Ph.D. in Food Science, Food Chemistry, Chemistry, Material Science, Biochemistry, Nutrition or a related field is required. The ideal candidate will show promise of or have already developed a national and international reputation for their innovative scholarship. Applications and Starting Date: The anticipated starting date is July 202 7 or as negotiated.  Qualified applicants should submit 1) a cover letter briefly summarizing background, qualifications, and interest in the position; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) a research statement outlining experience, interests, and goals, 4) a teaching statement outlining experience, interests, and goals, 5) copies of four relevant publications; and 6) names and contact information for four references. Materials should be submitted online to: https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32785 . Applications received by October 25, 202 6 will be given full consideration. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. This is an in-person position. The successful candidate will be expected to live within commuting distance of Ithaca, NY. Relocation assistance may be provided. Finalists for this position will be asked to sign an affidavit that they have not been found to have violated employer policies at their previous institution(s). Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointments to academic positions require candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. We seek a colleague with an outstanding record demonstrating success and promise across all these areas, and who will be supported by and contribute to a vibrant culture of inclusive excellence at Cornell. Learn how CALS fosters an environment where faculty are supported, valued, and empowered to make meaningful contributions throughout their careers Please forward questions to the search chair ( Robin Dando , robin.dando @cornell.edu ) or the search administrator (Patti Wojcik, pmw27@cornell.edu ). In compliance with New York's Pay Transparency Law, the annual base salary range is $90,000 - $135,000 when hired at assistant professor rank. This pay range represents base pay (for a 9-month appointment). Cornell University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Cornell University seeks to meet the needs of dual career couples, has a Dual Career program, and is a member of the Upstate New York Higher Education Recruitment Consortium to assist with dual career searches including positions available in higher education in the upstate New York area. The new faculty member will join a collaborative, interdisciplinary community on the main campus in Ithaca, New York.  Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League and Land-grant university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students, and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery, and engagement. Cornell’s regional and global presence includes state-wide Cornell Cooperative Extension programs and offices in all counties and boroughs, global partnerships with institutions and communities engaged in life-changing research and education, the medical college’s campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, and the Cornell Tech campus on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. With a founding principle of “any person, any study,” Cornell is an equal opportunity employer . Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-01 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-111535": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Postdoctoral Associate Postdoctoral Associate Academic Job Description Ashley School of Global Development & the Environment Postdoctoral Associate The College of Agriculture and Life Sciences (CALS) is a pioneer of purpose-driven science and Cornell University’s second largest college. We work across disciplines to tackle the challenges of our time through world-renowned research, education, and outreach. The questions we probe and the answers we seek focus on three overlapping concerns: We believe that achieving next-generation scientific breakthroughs requires an understanding of the world’s complex, interlocking systems. We believe that access to nutritious food and a healthy environment is a fundamental human right. We believe that ensuring a prosperous global future depends on the ability to support local people and communities everywhere. By working in and across multiple scientific areas, CALS can address challenges and opportunities of the greatest relevance, here in New York, across the nation, and around the world. Position Function The Ashley School of Global Development and the Environment at Cornell University seeks a highly motivated Postdoctoral Associate to lead the synthesis and pathways component of the multi-institutional Grassland Futures project. This North American initiative brings together researchers and conservation practitioners from organizations including: The Nature Conservancy and World Wildlife Fund and multiple universities to understand the drivers of grassland conversion across the Great Plains of the United States and Canada. The Postdoctoral Associate will work closely with an interdisciplinary team and diverse stakeholders to integrate diverse forms of knowledge and develop systems-based understanding of grass conservation challenges and opportunities and identify potential pathways for sustaining thriving grassland agroecosystems. This position is an exceptional opportunity for a scholar seeking to develop expertise at the interface of science, stakeholder engagement, and decision-making, while helping shape the future of grassland conservation across North America. There is a strong preference for this position to be based in Ithaca. Remote arrangements may be negotiable. Key areas of responsibility include: ●        Leading the development of qualitative and quantitative systems models that synthesize ecological, social, economic and policy drivers of grassland conversion and retention. ●        Designing and facilitating participatory workshops and collaborative scenario-planning processes with researchers and stakeholders. ●        Synthesizing findings from project workstreams, including spatial analyses, case studies, literature reviews and stakeholder engagement activities. ●        Co-developing future pathways and conservation scenarios that support grassland retention and sustainable agricultural landscapes. ●        Publishing research findings and contributing to policy briefs, practitioner resources and other decision-support products. ●        Collaborating with a large interdisciplinary team spanning academic, NGO and practitioner communities. Anticipated Division of Time ●        60% Systems modeling, synthesis and scenario development ●        20% Stakeholder engagement, workshop facilitation and collaborative planning ●        20% Scientific writing, publication development and project coordination Requirements We are seeking a highly motivated and collaborative scholar who has: ●        A Ph.D. (completed by start date) in natural resources, sustainability science, land systems science, geography, complex systems science, natural resource economics, ecology, environmental policy or a related field. ●        Strong interest and enthusiasm for interdisciplinary research on coupled human-natural systems, conservation, sustainability, or related topics. ●        Strong quantitative and analytical skills, including proficiency in one or more programming environments, such as R or Python. ●        Experience working on a project that involved synthesis of evidence and data from multiple disciplines. ●        Experience developing or applying systems-based approaches such as system dynamics, causal modeling, scenario analysis, participatory modeling, agent based modeling, or related frameworks. ●        Excellent written and oral communication skills. ●        Experience communicating scientific concepts to diverse audiences. ●        Strong organizational and project management skills, including the ability to work independently while coordinating with multiple collaborators. We are especially interested in candidates who are: ●        Curious and eager to learn from diverse perspectives. ●        Enthusiastic about tackling complex socio-environmental challenges. ●        Skilled at building relationships across disciplines, sectors and communities. ●        Comfortable facilitating conversations among stakeholders with different goals and viewpoints. ●        Open-minded about different forms of knowledge and evidence. ●        Motivated to produce research that can inform real-world conservation decision-making. Supervision Exercised There are no supervisory responsibilities for this position. To apply: Please apply via Academic Jobs Online ( https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33003) Qualified candidates should submit a short cover letter, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references via the website. Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointments to academic positions require candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. Applications will be reviewed as received, continuing until a suitable applicant is identified. CALS hiring range for this title is $63,480 - $66,000.00. College of Agriculture and Life Sciences Life. Changing. Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League and Land-grant university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students, and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery, and engagement. Cornell’s regional and global presence includes state-wide Cornell Cooperative Extension programs and offices in all counties and boroughs, global partnerships with institutions and communities engaged in life-changing research and education, the medical college’s campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, and the Cornell Tech campus on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. With a founding principle of “any person, any study,” Cornell is an equal opportunity employer . Pay Range: $62,232.00 - $88,745.00 Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-08 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-111528": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate or Full Professor (Scenic & Lighting Design)",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Assistant, Associate or Full Professor (Scenic & Lighting Design) Assistant, Associate or Full Professor (Scenic & Lighting Design) Cornell University’s Department of Performing and Media Arts seeks an Assistant, Associate or Full Professor in Scenic and Lighting Design for a tenure-track position beginning July 1, 2027. We are looking for a dynamic and innovative designer to instruct and mentor undergraduate students in our major and minor in Performing and Media Arts. Our interdisciplinary department teaches theory and practice in theatre, film, dance, performance, installation, and devised work, in increasingly unique and experimental ways. We offer B.A. and Ph.D. degrees in a liberal arts environment that allows a wide range of students to study and explore live and mediated forms of creativity, expression, and collaboration. Our ideal candidate will have experience teaching and working across different areas (i.e. scenic and lighting design; scenic and projection design; live and mediated performance). The successful candidate will teach the equivalent of two courses each semester, some of which may be as a designer on departmental productions. The candidate’s courses, to be determined by departmental needs and the individual’s particular area(s) of expertise, might include Production Design, Set Design, Worldbuilding, Lighting for Theatre and Dance, Lighting for Film, and/or Projection Design. Teaching responsibilities will also include mentorship of students in various roles in department-generated and student-generated productions. We are interested in recruiting candidates who have demonstrated an ongoing, successful engagement with the highest standards of teaching and professional creative activity, consistent with the expectations for excellence in a Research I university. To that end, colleagues are expected to continue developing their professional career beyond Cornell with a national and/or international profile. The Department of Performing and Media Arts is committed to the development of a campus climate that supports equality and diversity. More information is available at https://pma.cornell.edu . Interested applicants should submit the following materials to AJO: https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32702 :  a cover letter describing their practice, successful collaborations, and approach to teaching rooted in inclusive pedagogies. Candidates should also provide a curriculum vitae, a design portfolio, and three letters of recommendation. Candidate must have either an MFA or Ph.D officially awarded no later than July 1, 2026. The deadline for applications is November 15, 2026. [Pay Range: $110,800 - $240,000] Actual salary offers in the College of Arts and Sciences will be based on education, experience, discipline, and relevant skills. Disclosure: Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointments to academic positions require candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-09-21 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
