    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-112111": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Research Associate Research Associate Academic Job Description Department of Microbiology Research Associate The College of Agriculture and Life Sciences (CALS) is a pioneer of purpose-driven science and Cornell University’s second largest college. We work across disciplines to tackle the challenges of our time through world-renowned research, education, and outreach. The questions we probe and the answers we seek focus on three overlapping concerns: We believe that achieving next-generation scientific breakthroughs requires an understanding of the world’s complex, interlocking systems. We believe that access to nutritious food and a healthy environment is a fundamental human right. We believe that ensuring a prosperous global future depends on the ability to support local people and communities everywhere. By working in and across multiple scientific areas, CALS can address challenges and opportunities of the greatest relevance, here in New York, across the nation, and around the world. Position Function The core function of the position will be to conduct research on the regulation of bacterial development and antibiotic production in Dr. Kelley Gallagher’s laboratory in Cornell University’s Department of Microbiology. Ongoing projects are focused on understanding how the process of sporulation is regulated in the genus Streptomyces , examining how these regulatory pathways have evolved in the Phylum Actinomycetota, and leveraging this knowledge to manipulate antibiotic production. The Department of Microbiology in the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences at Cornell University has eleven active research laboratories. It serves as the home Department for the Graduate Field of Microbiology (with >40 affiliated faculty members) and provides a center of expertise for numerous aspects of microbial biology. The faculty and staff offer numerous advanced courses for both undergraduate and graduate level students. Research within the Department is focused on Molecular and Environmental Microbiology and includes specific expertise in molecular genetics, gene regulation, genomics, microbial cell biology, bioremediation, symbionts, pathogens, and ecosystems. The Research Associate position will contribute to at least one of the active projects involving the study of Streptomyces development. Approaches may include molecular genetics, bacterial physiology, genomics and biochemistry. The Research Associate will also be responsible for maintenance and operation of lab equipment necessary for completion of research projects, and provide insight into emerging opportunities that will facilitate or expedite research. This is a 85% FTE position  located in Ithaca, New York. This is a one-year appointment with possible extension depending on funding and performance. Anticipated Division of Time Research in Streptomyces development (80%) Conduct independent or collaborative research on Streptomyces development. Publications and presentations (15%) Prepare oral and written progress reports and contribute to the writing of manuscripts. Laboratory maintenance (5%) Maintain standard operating protocols (SOPs). Maintain supplies of consumables necessary for daily operation of key instrumentation and processes. Place orders as needed. Requirements Ph.D. in Microbiology or related field. 3+ years of experience in postdoctoral training. Demonstrated experience and strong publication record in microbiology or related field. Strong interpersonal, oral and written communication skills. Supervision Exercised The Research Associate will supervise graduate students, undergraduate students, and visiting scientists involved in research on various projects. To apply: Please apply via Academic Jobs Online ( https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo?job-3277-33258 ) Qualified candidates should submit a short cover letter, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references via the website. Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointments to academic positions require candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. Applications will be reviewed as received, continuing until a suitable applicant is identified. CALS hiring rate for this title is $66,300 - $68,000 (85% FTE Salary) College of Agriculture and Life Sciences Life. Changing. Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League and Land-grant university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students, and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery, and engagement. Cornell’s regional and global presence includes state-wide Cornell Cooperative Extension programs and offices in all counties and boroughs, global partnerships with institutions and communities engaged in life-changing research and education, the medical college’s campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, and the Cornell Tech campus on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. With a founding principle of “any person, any study,” Cornell is an equal opportunity employer . Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-27 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-112093": {
      "title": "Teaching Professor in Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Teaching Professor in Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Teaching Professor in Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering A description of the teaching faculty titles and expectations as well as the reason for their adoption is listed at https://deanoffaculty.cornell.edu/faculty-governance/archives/resolutions/resolution-201-approving-cornell-engineerings-use-of-the-teaching-professor-titles/ .  Teaching faculty at Cornell hold renewable, multi-year contracts and typically teach two classes per term. The Sibley School of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering at Cornell University, Ithaca, New York, USA invites applications for teaching faculty at all levels (Assistant Teaching Professor, Associate Teaching Professor, or Teaching Professor) in Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering (MAE). Candidates must have a doctoral degree in Mechanical Engineering or Aerospace Engineering or a closely related field as well as the ability to teach multiple courses in the core undergraduate and Masters-level MAE curriculum. We expect teaching faculty to bring passion and excellence broadly to the curriculum, but the department also particularly seeks candidates who are excited to contribute to our teaching endeavor in Energy, Design, Manufacturing, or Aerospace. In 1868, Ezra Cornell wrote, “I would found an institution where any person can find instruction in any study,” and we seek faculty members who share this historical commitment.  Applicants at the Associate and full teaching professor ranks should have a demonstrated track record of excellence in instruction and curricular development. Applicants at the Assistant Teaching Professor level should show a pattern of early-career training in instruction such as teaching assistantships, training, workshops and/or conferences. Salary and rank are commensurate with qualifications and experience. How to apply and what to submit: The anticipated starting date is 1 July 2027 or as negotiated. Qualified applicants should submit 1) a cover letter briefly summarizing background, qualifications, and interest in the position, 2) a Curriculum Vitae, 3) a teaching statement outlining experience, interests, goals, and techniques, and 4) names and contact information for three references. Materials should be submitted online to: https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32812 . Applications received by November 15 will be given full consideration. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Inquires may be directed to: mae_teach_prof26@cornell.edu Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointment to academic positions requires candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. Pay Ranges: $100,100 - $237,300 The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: ● Prior relevant work or industry experience. ● Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. ● Academic Discipline (faculty pay ranges reflects 9-month annual salary) ● Unique applicable skills. Cornell University seeks to meet the needs of dual career couples, has a Dual Career program, and is a member of the Upstate New York Higher Education Recruitment Consortium to assist with dual career searches including positions available in higher education in the upstate New York area. Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-27 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-112084": {
      "title": "Academic Career Opportunities",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Post Dr Assoc Post Dr Assoc Academic Job Description SIPS-Plant Breeding & Genetics Postdoctoral Associate The College of Agriculture and Life Sciences (CALS) is a pioneer of purpose-driven science and Cornell University’s second largest college. We work across disciplines to tackle the challenges of our time through world-renowned research, education, and outreach. The questions we probe and the answers we seek focus on three overlapping concerns: We believe that achieving next-generation scientific breakthroughs requires an understanding of the world’s complex, interlocking systems. We believe that access to nutritious food and a healthy environment is a fundamental human right. We believe that ensuring a prosperous global future depends on the ability to support local people and communities everywhere. By working in and across multiple scientific areas, CALS can address challenges and opportunities of the greatest relevance, here in New York, across the nation, and around the world. The CALS School of Integrative of Plant Science (SIPS) encompasses five fields: Horticulture, Plant Biology, Plant Breeding & Genetics, Plant Pathology and Plant-Microbe Biology, and Soil and Crop Sciences. Combined there are 100+ faculty, ~200 undergraduate majors, 200+ graduate students in six fields, and 600+ other employees located in Ithaca, Geneva, and other satellite locations across New York State. The School offers the multidisciplinary undergraduate major in the Plant Sciences and is also home to the Agricultural Sciences major. School faculty also contribute to the Biology, Global Development, and Viticulture & Enology majors. Position Function This position requires an experienced (>2 years) researcher to develop a study on perceptions, preferences and adoption behaviors of US farmers towards a novel biotechnology product. The Postdoctoral Associate will be required to design, implement and analyze state-wide (NY and California) surveys of farmers using behavioral economics methods in the field to gauge receptivity, risk perception, preferences and tradeoffs associated with adoption of a new biotech product. The Postdoctoral Associate will also lead the design, implementation and analysis of a nation-wide survey around motivations and mechanisms for US organic farmer engagement in participatory plant breeding and research. The Postdoctoral Associate will work with a graduate student for both of these projects who will be conducting related research. This is an in-person position. The successful candidate will be expected to relocate within commuting distance of Ithaca, NY. Anticipated Division of Time Research method development 20% Field research 20% Data analysis 30% Writing 20% Student Support 10% Requirements Required: PhD in Behavioral Economics, Agricultural Economics or similar field Experience working with primary data such as national agricultural datasets and/or census data. Experience designing, implementing and analyzing surveys with farmers Experience in quantitative econometric research design and data analysis, with a focus on causal inference and agricultural technology adoption. Strong analytical, organizational, communication, and problem-solving skills, attention to detail, and ability to handle multiple tasks simultaneously. Ability to travel within the US for fieldwork. Driving required, must have and maintain a valid and unrestricted NY State Driver’s License and be cleared for university business. Upon hire must register with Agile/Fleet Commander . Preferred: Experience using game-based, or product concept testing behavioral methods Demonstrated experience engaging with US commodity or specialty crop grower groups Demonstrated experience working in interdisciplinary teams with biophysical scientists Experience supporting and mentoring graduate students Supervision Exercised There are no supervisory responsibilities for this position. To apply: Please apply via Academic Jobs Online ( https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33257 ) . Qualified candidates should submit a short cover letter, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references via the website. Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointments to academic positions require candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. Applications will be reviewed as received, continuing until a suitable applicant is identified. CALS hiring range for this title is $63,480 to $65,000. College of Agriculture and Life Sciences Life. Changing. Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League and Land-grant university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students, and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery, and engagement. Cornell’s regional and global presence includes state-wide Cornell Cooperative Extension programs and offices in all counties and boroughs, global partnerships with institutions and communities engaged in life-changing research and education, the medical college’s campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, and the Cornell Tech campus on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. With a founding principle of “any person, any study,” Cornell is an equal opportunity employer . Pay Range: $62,232.00 - $88,745.00 Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-27 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-112074": {
      "title": "Extension Associate",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Extension Associate Extension Associate Soil and Crop Sciences Section School of Integrative Plant Science Senior Research Associate – Director Soil Health Laboratory The College of Agriculture and Life Sciences (CALS) is a pioneer of purpose-driven science and Cornell University’s second largest college. We work across disciplines to tackle the challenges of our time through world-renowned research, education, and outreach. The questions we probe and the answers we seek focus on three overlapping concerns: We believe that achieving next-generation scientific breakthroughs requires an understanding of the world’s complex, interlocking systems. We believe that access to nutritious food and a healthy environment is a fundamental human right. We believe that ensuring a prosperous global future depends on the ability to support local people and communities everywhere. By working in and across multiple scientific areas, CALS can address challenges and opportunities of the greatest relevance, here in New York, across the nation, and around the world. Position Function The Director of the Cornell Soil Health Laboratory provides strategic leadership and operational oversight for a nationally recognized laboratory dedicated to advancing comprehensive soil health assessment, research, and outreach. Building on the Laboratory’s mission to deliver integrated measurements of soil physical, chemical, and biological properties to support sustainable land management, the Director guides the development and implementation of innovative laboratory protocols and analytical services, manages high-throughput soil testing operations, and guides the continued development and expansion of the Cornell Assessment of Soil Health framework. The role integrates administration, applied research, and extension by supervising staff, coordinating multi-site laboratory activities, fostering collaborations with Cornell faculty and external partners, and communicating actionable soil health insights through workshops, publications, and field engagement. The Director will integrate with the broader soil health ecosystem at Cornell, including research, teaching, and extension programs. The Director also drives continuous improvement of analytical offerings, oversees program finances and client services, and contributes to education and training that translates soil health science into practice for agricultural and environmental stakeholders. This is an in-person position. The successful candidate will be expected to relocate within commuting distance of Ithaca, NY. Anticipated Division of Time Program Leadership & Laboratory Operations (60%) : Provide overall direction and management of the Soil Health Laboratory, including oversight of daily operations, supervision of technical staff, coordination of sample processing workflows, and ensuring efficient high-throughput delivery of soil health assessments. Strategic Program Development & Administration (20%): Oversee program growth, including budgeting, pricing structures, client services, and long-term planning to sustain and expand the laboratory’s impact and service capacity. Analytical Development & Research Integration (10%): Guide development and refinement of soil health metrics, methodologies, and new analytical offerings within the Cornell Soil Health Assessment framework, while collaborating with faculty and researchers to advance soil health science. Education & Training (10%): Contribute to formal and informal education through teaching, training, and outreach in soil health methods. Audiences include Cornell staff, students, and stakeholders. Topics of instruction and outreach will include experiential learning related to soil health measurement, soil management, and soil health testing. Requirements The successful applicant will have a Ph.D. in Soil Science, Agronomy, Environmental Science or a related discipline, with strong experience related to the measurement of soil physical, chemical, and biological properties and processes. The candidate will have experience with laboratory methodologies, analytical protocols, and quality assurance systems. The successful candidate will have strong project management and organizational skills. Other desirable competencies include: Expertise in soil health assessment methods and interpretation Experience managing laboratory operations and/or high-throughput testing systems Demonstrated ability to supervise technical personnel Experience managing program finances, budget development, fiscal planning, and resource allocation Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with faculty, researchers, and external partners Familiarity with laboratory information systems and operational workflows Supervision Exercised The Director will supervise a team of technicians and research support specialists. To apply: Please apply via Academic Jobs Online ( https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33233 ) Qualified candidates should submit a short cover letter, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references via the website. Applications will be reviewed as received, continuing until a suitable applicant is identified. For full consideration submit before December 7, 2026. Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointments to academic positions require candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. CALS hiring range for this title is $102,000 - $110,000. College of Agriculture and Life Sciences Life. Changing. Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League and Land-grant university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students, and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery, and engagement. Cornell’s regional and global presence includes state-wide Cornell Cooperative Extension programs and offices in all counties and boroughs, global partnerships with institutions and communities engaged in life-changing research and education, the medical college’s campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, and the Cornell Tech campus on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. With a founding principle of “any person, any study,” Cornell is an equal opportunity employer . Employment Assistance: For specific questions about the position or application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or for general questions email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRRA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline (faculty pay ranges reflects 9-month annual salary unless otherwise noted) Unique applicable skills. Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-27 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
