    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-112072": {
      "title": "Director, Sample Preparation and Spatial Technologies Facility, Biotechnology Resource Center (BRC)",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Director, Sample Preparation and Spatial Technologies Facility, Biotechnology Resource Center (BRC) Director, Sample Preparation and Spatial Technologies Facility, Biotechnology Resource Center (BRC) Director, Sample Preparation and Spatial Technologies Facility, Biotechnology Resource Center (BRC) University Title: Research Scientist Cornell Institute of Biotechnology Research and Innovation The Biotechnology Resource Center (BRC) is seeking a Director for the Sample Preparation and Spatial Technologies Facility. This new core facility will provide a staffed, shared laboratory where Cornell researchers in the life sciences can access specialized instrumentation, reagents, protocols, and scientific expertise needed to prepare biological samples for advanced analysis. A central goal is to enable researchers who may not have the necessary expertise, infrastructure, or instrumentation within their own labs to adopt new experimental approaches through hands-on access, consultation, and training from BRC scientific staff. The facility will serve as a “front door” to the BRC, helping researchers design and implement sample preparation workflows that connect seamlessly with downstream analytical capabilities across BRC core facilities, including genomics and epigenomics, proteomics and metabolomics, imaging, flow cytometry, and bioinformatics. Supported activities will encompass a broad range of sample preparation workflows as well as emerging and technically complex approaches that span multiple core facilities, including single-cell and spatial omics technologies. By bridging individual research laboratories with the BRC’s analytical cores, the facility will lower barriers to adopting sophisticated experimental methods, promote standardized and reproducible workflows, and accelerate discovery across the life sciences. The mission of the facility is to Share technical expertise and provide broad access to specialized equipment, reagents, protocols, and other resources, while promoting efficient use and maintenance of shared instrumentation. Lower barriers to adoption of innovative technologies by making sophisticated experimental workflows accessible to investigators across agriculture, veterinary medicine, engineering, and the biological and biomedical sciences. Facilitate integrated experimental workflows that connect sample preparation with analytical capabilities across multiple BRC core facilities. Create a vibrant, collaborative environment for the development, optimization, evaluation, and adoption of innovative technologies. The Director will provide leadership, management, and strategic vision for the facility. In addition to overseeing wet laboratory spaces and facility operations, the Director will actively support research through consultation, training, technology development, and collaboration with faculty, staff, and trainees. The Director may participate in collaborative research projects and extramural proposals as PI, co-PI, or co-investigator, as appropriate. The Director will work closely with researchers and units across multiple colleges to understand evolving community needs, develop new capabilities, and build a strong and diverse user community. The Director will work closely with the BRC Director, the facility advisory board, and directors of other BRC core facilities to coordinate services, develop integrated workflows, and contribute to strategic planning across the BRC. Characteristic Duties and Responsibilities: Provide scientific and operational leadership for the Sample Preparation and Spatial Technologies Facility and supervise facility personnel. Oversee shared laboratory spaces and ensure safe, efficient, and effective operation of facility instrumentation and resources. Provide consultation to researchers on experimental design, sample preparation strategies, selection of appropriate technologies, and integration with downstream BRC analytical services. Develop and provide introductory and advanced hands-on training for users in sample preparation workflows, specialized instrumentation, and shared laboratory practices. Work collaboratively with other BRC core directors and staff to develop workflows that span multiple technologies and core facilities, including single-cell and spatial applications. Manage cost recovery operations, including development of service and access rates, oversight of facility budget, and forecasting of facility usage and resource needs. Assess emerging technologies and community needs and identify opportunities for acquisition or development of new capabilities in coordination with facility stakeholders. Lead or collaborate on instrumentation and research proposals, including NIH S10 and other shared instrumentation applications, as PI or co-PI where appropriate. Prepare and present operating, scientific, and strategic reports to facility faculty advisory board and BRC leadership. Promote facility capabilities and build the user community through engagement with Cornell and external researchers and coordination with BRC training, consultation, outreach, and communications activities. Maintain and develop a portfolio of instrumentation, technologies, protocols, reagents, and associated software responsive to evolving research needs. Promote rigorous, reproducible, and safe laboratory practices across facility-supported workflows. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in biology, chemistry, biochemistry, molecular biology, biomedical engineering, or related life sciences discipline and recognized stature in their field. Knowledge of life sciences laboratory operations, including laboratory organization, instrumentation, safety, and best practices for shared research environments. Significant experience with molecular biology and genomics workflows, including DNA and RNA extraction, PCR, sequencing library preparation, nucleotide sequencing, epigenomic and chromatin profiling assays, or related areas. Direct experience operating next-generation sequencing platforms, including library pooling, sequencing recipe design, and library quality control. Experience with one or more additional areas relevant to the facility, such as protein or proteomic sample preparation, flow cytometry, imaging, single-cell methods, spatial biology, cell and tissue processing, preparation of diverse sample types (e.g., frozen mammalian tissues, cell lines, plants, yeast, and bacteria), or other advanced biological technologies. Demonstrated ability to learn, evaluate, optimize, and implement new experimental technologies and workflows. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with a broad range of researchers, including faculty, postdoctoral researchers, graduate students, and others, to provide scientific consultation and training. Demonstrated understanding of research core or shared resource facility operations. Strong commitment to the mission of shared research facilities and to enabling research across diverse scientific disciplines. Supervisory and operational management experience, including training and mentoring technical staff, students, and trainees. Preferred Qualifications: Experience establishing, optimizing, and delivering full-service assays in a core facility setting, including protocol optimization, quality control, and technical staff training. Experience with multi-omic assays and with custom library sequencing services. Active engagement with the shared resource facility community (e.g., Association of Biomolecular Resource Facilities), including leading working groups or contributing to technical guidelines and best practices. Teaching and outreach experience at the undergraduate, graduate, or professional level, including laboratory instruction, curriculum development, and mentoring of student researchers. Experience preparing grant and equipment proposals to support laboratory instrumentation and research infrastructure. Record of peer-reviewed publications in molecular biology, genomics, or related fields, including published methods or protocols. Experience presenting technical and scientific results at national and regional meetings. The initial appointment is a 3-year term renewable contingent upon continued funding and successful performance. This is an Endowed position. Hiring Range for this Position: $112,000 - $116,000 The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. The Institute of Biotechnology is a dynamic hub for life sciences research at Cornell University. The Institute ( https://www.biotech.cornell.edu )  offers cutting edge biotechnologies, expertise in applications,  technology testing and development and consultation for life sciences  researchers at Cornell University and outside institutions. The  Institute houses the Biotechnology Resource Center (BRC), which offers  shared core facilities services to catalyze and promote life sciences  research, and is involved with many intercampus initiatives and  multi-institutional collaborations. In addition to Sample Preparation  and Spatial Technologies, the BRC includes core facility operations in  Genomics, Epigenomics, Proteomics and Metabolomics, Bioinformatics,  Imaging, and Flow Cytometry.  The Institute also administers the Center  for Advanced Technology (CAT) in the Life Sciences to assist life  science researchers in the development of biotechnologies with  commercial potential in partnership with New York State industries, and  the Center for Life Science Ventures, a state-of- the-art business  incubator for emerging biotechnology companies.  With its extensive  network of academic, federal, not‐for‐profit enterprises and commercial  partners, the BRC serves as a vital partner in life sciences research  and innovation. Institute  of Biotechnology staff enjoy working in a collaborative and innovative  organization within Cornell University, an award-winning employer  recognized for supporting employee health, workplace diversity,  work-life fit, and environmental sustainability.  Cornell University  offers competitive compensation and benefits.  The Institute is located  on the main Ithaca campus, in the heart of the Finger Lakes of central  New York State.  Ithaca and the surrounding area is noted for high  quality of living surrounded by natural beauty and a vibrant community. Why Cornell? Benefits and rewards include: Comprehensive health care options, generous retirement contributions, access to wellness programs , employee discounts with local and national retail brands, and more. Our  leave provisions include three weeks of vacation and 13 holidays:  Martin Luther King, Jr. Day, Memorial Day, Juneteenth, Independence Day,  Labor Day, Thanksgiving and the day after, and an end of the year  winter break from December 25-January 1. Impressive  educational benefits include tuition-free Part-Time Study and Employee  Degree Program, heavily discounted eCornell courses and certificate  programs, tuition aid for external education, and Cornell Children's  Tuition Assistance Program. Position needed to start as soon as possible. Applications should be submitted by November 2, 2026 . For full consideration, qualified applicants should submit: 1) Cover letter summarizing background, qualifications, and interest in the position 2) Curriculum Vitae 3) Names and contact information for three references Materials should be submitted online here: https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33240 Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-26 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-112068": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, Jewish Studies Program",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Postdoctoral Associate, Jewish Studies Program Postdoctoral Associate, Jewish Studies Program The Jewish Studies Program at Cornell University invites applications for a two-year Postdoctoral Associate position in Jewish Studies of Contemporary North America, beginning July 1, 2027. We welcome applications from Jewish Studies scholars working in any discipline or across disciplines in the Humanities and Social Sciences whose scholarship focuses on North America from the mid-Twentieth Century to the present. This is a residential postdoctoral position. Postdoctoral Associates are expected to teach two courses per year on topics within the field of Jewish Studies, including one introductory-level course and one advanced seminar. For the first year the teaching load will be 0-2: in Fall 2027, the Fellow will be in residence conducting research; and in Spring 2028 the Fellow will teach two courses. In 2028-2029, the Fellow will teach one course per semester. The successful candidate will pursue an independent research agenda, contribute to the intellectual life of the Jewish Studies Program, and deliver a public lecture. Full consideration will be given to applications submitted by January 15, 2027. Applications will be considered until the position is filled. Candidates who do not have the Ph.D. in hand at the time of application must include a letter from the committee chair or department stating that the candidate will have completed all degree requirements before the start date of the postdoc position; the actual degree conferral date can be after the appointment start date. To apply, candidates should submit the following application materials via Academic Jobs Online, job #33112 : 1. Information cover sheet (will be generated via your AJO account): (a) Full name and home institution (b) Date (or expected date) of Ph.D. (c) Research interest(s) (d) Referees’ names and emails 2. Cover letter 3. Curriculum vitae 4. Course proposal with sample syllabus for introductory 2000-level course in Jewish Studies. 5. Course proposal with sample syllabus for upper 4000-level seminar in Jewish Studies. 6. One writing sample [30-page limit] 7. Three letters of recommendation. Please ask referees to upload their letters directly through the link provided by Academic Jobs Online. Pay Range : Salary will be at least $63,480 Actual salary offers in the College of Arts and Sciences will be based on education, experience, discipline, and relevant skills. Further Info: https://jewishstudies.cornell.edu/ Search Committee <jsp-jobs@cornell.edu> Search Committee Cornell University 409 White Hall Ithaca, NY 14853 Pay Range: $62,232.00 - $88,745.00 Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Stat