    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-112163": {
      "title": "Dual Rank - Extension Associate / Senior Extension Associate - Climate Jobs Associate",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Dual Rank - Extension Associate / Senior Extension Associate - Climate Jobs Associate Dual Rank - Extension Associate / Senior Extension Associate - Climate Jobs Associate The Climate Jobs Institute is based in New York, NY, within the Cornell University ILR School located at 570 Lexington Ave. The Opportunity: The School of Industrial and Labor Relations at Cornell University has an immediate opening for Climate Jobs Associate - Extension Associate / Senior Extension Associate in the Climate Jobs Institute in the Extension / Outreach Division with a focus on the intersection of labor, employment, climate justice and climate change. These positions are responsible for developing and delivering programs and services that are closely aligned with the strategic mission and core values of the ILR School, and for monitoring quality, impact, and outcomes of education, training, technical assistance, consulting and research activities of the program. The successful candidate for this position will help lead the organization's strategic direction, including identifying and prioritizing new areas of work that are politically relevant and of particular interest to CJI’s union partners and allies. The role will help build and maintain strong, trusting relationships with union-led climate jobs coalitions as well as individual building trades, energy unions, and other parts of the labor movement. The successful candidate will also be a leader on the CJI team, helping build an organizational culture grounded in excellent, impactful work, fun, flexibility, career development, and respect. The successful candidate will have at least ten years of experience working with the U.S. labor movement, including facilitating labor convenings, providing training and education programs to union leaders and members, and interacting with high-level local, state, and national union leaders. Excellent judgment and skill in navigating sensitive and complex issues with labor partners and allies are essential. This position will be responsible for understanding and supporting the needs of state-based climate jobs coalitions. This includes meeting with coalition and union leaders to understand the challenges they’re facing and working closely with them to design and deliver educational programming that addresses those challenges and helps find solutions to advance a union clean energy economy. The candidate will have an excellent understanding of the challenges and opportunities of working with unions on climate and clean energy issues. This includes a strong understanding of building trades and energy unions culture, structure, and core interests. Experience with union training programs, including apprenticeship and pre-apprenticeship programs, is also important, as is the potential to expand pathways from underserved communities to union careers and to build relationships between unions and workforce development, environmental justice, and climate organizations. The successful candidate will be able to help union partners establish and expand new pre-apprenticeship programs with a specific focus on climate and clean energy. Knowledge of and experience in securing and applying labor and equity standards, such as prevailing wage, Project Labor Agreements, labor peace, apprenticeship utilization, and community workforce agreements, is also essential to this position. The position will represent CJI to external partners, including unions, climate groups, policymakers, and other labor allies. This position will also help ensure CJI's financial sustainability and success by identifying new funding opportunities and ensuring it delivers excellent, high-quality work to existing funding partners. Specifically, the Extension Associate / Senior Extension Associate will: ·         Align CJI’s work priorities and programs to the core needs and interests of the labor movement, especially as it relates to unions’ advocacy for climate action and building a union clean energy economy. ·         Build and maintain strong working relationships with high-level union leaders and staff, the Climate Jobs National Resource Center, and climate jobs coalitions. ·         Design and deliver educational programming to unions, climate jobs coalitions, and other partners on key climate and clean energy issues with the goal of expanding union clean energy jobs and unions’ advocacy for strong climate action. ·         Identify opportunities to expand climate and clean energy training programs within the U.S. union structure with the specific goal of expanding pathways from frontline, environmental justice to union clean energy careers. ·         Work closely with unions and climate jobs coalitions to identify opportunities to establish or expand union pre-apprenticeship programs, including programs with a specific climate and clean energy dimension. ·         In collaboration with key partners, identify research priorities and projects that examine the labor and employment impacts of key climate and energy issues and provide findings to inform the development of union- and equity-centered climate policy. ·         Stay informed and well-versed about a broad range of best practices and/or advances in knowledge in the field of labor, climate, clean energy, and workforce development and equity. ·         Develop and maintain a network of relationships that are drawn upon to build an understanding of the major forces shaping the needs, positions, and actions of unions, climate jobs coalitions, climate organizations, and other labor allies. About Us Work touches nearly all of us — but the relationships between workers, employers, and our economy are more complex than ever. At the School of Industrial and Labor Relations, we’re at the heart of these critical intersections driving innovations that will define the future of work. More information about the ILR School can be obtained at our web site, http://www.ilr.cornell.edu Our Team The Climate Jobs Institute at Cornell University’s ILR School is guiding New York’s and the nation’s transition to a strong and resilient clean energy economy that tackles the climate crisis and creates high-quality union jobs. As part of this mission, we are committed to fostering a collaborative and welcoming workplace where all individuals feel respected and supported. We embrace inclusive practices that engage a wide range of communities and perspectives, believing that meaningful change is built through shared purpose, open dialogue, and a culture of belonging that strengthens our collective impact. Through cutting-edge policy research, deep relationships with on-the-ground partners, and innovative training and education programs, the Institute provides the information that policymakers, the labor and environmental movements, industry leaders and others need to navigate this historic transition to a zero-carbon economy. The Climate Jobs Institute serves as the academic and educational partner to the Climate Jobs National Resource Center, providing research, policy and educational support to coalitions of unions in numerous U.S. states. More information about the Climate Jobs Institute can be found on the website https://www.ilr.cornell.edu/climate-jobs-institute Extension Associate / Senior Extension Associate is a non-tenure track, academic term position for an initial term ending June 30, 2028. Reappointment for additional terms is possible based on successful performance, available work and continued funding, as well as recommendations by the appropriate Executive Director and Senior Associate Dean for Research, Outreach, and External Relations. What we need: Master's or other advanced degree in related subject matter expertise (industrial and labor relations, employment or labor law, workforce development) with relevant expertise related to labor unions, social and economic imbalances, and climate change. Minimum of 2 to 5 years of experience working with the U.S. labor movement or on climate change with an emphasis on worker, labor or social justice issues for extension associate . Minimum of 7 to 10 years of experience working with the U.S. labor movement or on climate change with an emphasis on worker, labor or social justice issues for senior extension associate . Significant experience with presenting and adult education training, including but not limited to facilitating labor convenings, providing training and education programs to union leaders and members, training in principles of adult education and working with adult learners. Demonstrated ability to design and deliver participatory curriculum for the highest level of union leadership. Demonstrated relevant professional leadership and network development at the state, regional, national, and/or international areas in relevant professional, trade, policy, or public networks, and ability to interact with high-level local, state, and national union leaders. Excellent judgment and skill in navigating sensitive and complex issues with labor partners and allies. Demonstrated practical experience designing and advancing pro-worker, pro-union public policy. Strong skills in written communication, report writing, quantitative, qualitative, geospatial analysis, and policy research and analysis. Demonstrated experience in leading teams and mentoring, coaching, and supervising teaching/ training and/or research personnel and graduate students; five to seven years preferred. Ability to travel for educational and other custom programs and events. Model and support a culture of inclusion, belonging, and wellbeing and continually seek to understand how your role, behaviors, and actions impact the success of this culture. You will be a top candidate with our preferred qualifications: PhD or terminal degree in related field preferred for senior. Knowledge of and experience in securing and applying labor and equity standards, such as prevailing wage, Project Labor Agreements, labor peace, apprenticeship utilization, and community workforce agreements. Experience with union training programs, including pre-apprentice and apprentice programs. Experience working with two or more of the following functions: worker organizing, political organizing, policy/legislation, collective bargaining, representation, leadership development. Experience designing surveys and studies, ability to conduct qualitative interviews with labor, industry and governmental officials. Experience with federated labor bodies, including the national AFL-CIO, State AFL-CIO and/or Central Labor Council/Area Labor Federations. Experience in successful grant writing and grant management. Minimum of three to five years demonstrated experience in project management (coordination among stakeholders, staff, contractors; managing budgets and logistics, etc.). ILR School Outreach appreciates employees by providing the opportunity for a 3:2 campus-to-home hybrid schedule. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. In compliance with New York’s Pay Transparency Law, the annual base salary range for this open rank position is $90,000 - $125,000. Cornell University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Cornell appreciates employees with great rewards & benefits: Cornell provides great benefits that include educational benefits, access to a plethora of wellness programs, employee discounts with local and national retail brands, health care options to choose from, generous paid leave provisions and superior retirement contributions. Cornell has also been nationally recognized as an award-winning workplace for our health, wellbeing, sustainability, and diversity initiatives. For more information click here. To Apply: Interested parties should submit a vita and letter of application describing qualifications and research agenda and teaching/training experience. Application materials should be submitted through Academic Jobs Online: https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33282 This is an academic, full-time, non-tenure track benefits eligible term opportunity located in New York, NY, within the Cornell University ILR School located at 570 Lexington Ave. Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-29 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-112156": {
      "title": "Research Associate, Cornell SonicMEMS Laboratory",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Research Associate, Cornell SonicMEMS Laboratory Research Associate, Cornell SonicMEMS Laboratory Cornell University seeks applications for a Research Associate in Professor Amit Lal’s Cornell SonicMEMS Laboratory. This is a one-year full-time benefits-eligible position that may be extended for up to four more years depending on performance, continuation of funding, and continued eligibility to work in the United States. The SonicMEMS Lab works on diverse topics aimed at transforming the way world can be viewed. Topics include: (1) Gigahertz Ultrasonic for chip-scale communications, sensing, and computation, (2) Pyroelectric-based high voltage generators for gas ionization and detection, (3) NEMS scale mechanically actuated hyperspectral computation with ferroelectric weight storage (4) Biomedical sensing with CMOS compatible gigahertz ultrasound pulse-echo arrays, (5) Tunneling sensors for low power RF detection, and (6) Ultrasonic and magnetically stabilized levitation for IMU sensor isolation. Candidates will partner with Professor Lal in support of key strategic research initiatives and management of the research enterprise. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to: Serve as a research partner, drive experiments towards proposal development, analyze data. Manage and track research milestones, project schedules, action items, and sponsor deliverables to ensure timely and successful completion. Produce manuscripts, reports, abstracts for conferences, grant proposals. Manage the operations of the research group from training new members, overseeing the maintenance of some key equipment and ordering of supplies. To be eligible, candidates must: Have received a PhD with specialization in MEMS or a related science field. Demonstrate quantitative/qualitative research skills, excellent written and oral communication skills, and the ability to work independently and with collaborators. A track record of original first-author publications or conference proceedings from doctoral work is desirable. Have at least three years of experience in proposal writing and demonstrated success in obtaining awards. Travel independently or with team members to PI meetings, conferences, sponsor reviews, and other events as needed to support ongoing projects and future research opportunities. Contribute to and lead research proposal development in collaboration with faculty, industry partners, and other stakeholders to secure funding and expand the research program. How to apply and what to submit: The anticipated starting date is January 18, 2027 or as negotiated. Qualified applicants should submit 1) a cover letter briefly summarizing background, qualifications, and interest in the position, 2) a Curriculum Vitae, 3) a research statement outlining experience, interests, and goals, 4) copies of three relevant publications, and 6) names and contact information for three references. Materials should be submitted online to: https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33271 . Applications received by November 30, 2026 will be given full consideration. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Inquiries may be directed to: Professor Amit Lal, amit.lal@cornell.edu Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointment to academic positions requires candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. Pay Range: $50,900-$95,000. The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: ● Prior relevant work or industry experience ● Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position ● Academic Discipline ● Unique applicable skills Cornell University seeks to meet the needs of dual career couples, has a Dual Career program, and is a member of the Upstate New York Higher Education Recruitment Consortium to assist with dual career searches including positions available in higher education in the upstate New York area. Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-29 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-112206": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor in Indigenous Literatures",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Assistant/Associate Professor in Indigenous Literatures Assistant/Associate Professor in Indigenous Literatures The Department of Literatures in English and the American Indian & Indigenous Studies Program (AIISP) at Cornell University invite applications for an appointment in Indigenous Literatures at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. The appointment will begin July 1, 2027. Ph.D. in Literature, Indigenous studies, or related fields must be in hand prior to the appointment. The candidate will hold a joint appointment in the Department of Literatures in English and AIISP. We are open to all areas of specialty within Indigenous literatures, and are especially enthusiastic about candidates with expertise in Indigenous methodologies, literatures of the Northeast, treaty relations, and interdisciplinary approaches to ecocriticism and critical theory more broadly. We seek scholars who have ongoing connections to Indigenous communities. The successful candidate will contribute to shaping Indigenous Studies in the College of Arts and Sciences at Cornell, a land grant university located on the traditional homelands of the Gayogo̱hó:nǫʼ (Cayuga Nation). Teaching responsibilities (2/2 load) include core introductory courses in AIISP and Literatures in English, first-year writing seminars, as well as upper-level undergraduate courses and graduate seminars of your own design. This would include joining rotations for one or more AIISP courses, including: Indigenous North America, Indigenous Issues in Global Perspective, Introduction to Native American Literature, and the graduate seminar in Critical Approaches to American Indian and Indigenous Studies. Other responsibilities include supervision of undergraduate and graduate students, as well as service to the AIISP, the Department of Literatures in English, the College, and the University, appropriate to rank and attentive to the nature of the joint appointment. Please submit a letter of application addressed to Professors Elisha Cohn and Juliana Hu Pegues, c.v., research statement, statement of teaching philosophy, and one writing sample of no more than 30 pages to Academic Jobs Online position #33294 ( https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33294 ). Please note that letters of recommendation and additional writing samples may be requested as the search progresses. Questions may be directed to Elisha Cohn (ejc244@cornell.edu) and Juliana Hu Pegues (jhupegues@cornell.edu). -- Disclosure: Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointments to academic positions require candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. Pay Range: $83,200 - $140,000. Actual salary offers in the College of Arts and Sciences will be based on education, experience, discipline, and relevant skills. Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-30 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-112179": {
      "title": "Academic Career Opportunities",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Post Dr Assoc Post Dr Assoc Academic Job Description SIPS-Plant Breeding & Genetics Postdoctoral Associate The College of Agriculture and Life Sciences (CALS) is a pioneer of purpose-driven science and Cornell University’s second largest college. We work across disciplines to tackle the challenges of our time through world-renowned research, education, and outreach. The questions we probe and the answers we seek focus on three overlapping concerns: We believe that achieving next-generation scientific breakthroughs requires an understanding of the world’s complex, interlocking systems. We believe that access to nutritious food and a healthy environment is a fundamental human right. We believe that ensuring a prosperous global future depends on the ability to support local people and communities everywhere. By working in and across multiple scientific areas, CALS can address challenges and opportunities of the greatest relevance, here in New York, across the nation, and around the world. The CALS School of Integrative of Plant Science (SIPS) encompasses five fields: Horticulture, Plant Biology, Plant Breeding & Genetics, Plant Pathology and Plant-Microbe Biology, and Soil and Crop Sciences. Combined there are 100+ faculty, ~200 undergraduate majors, 200+ graduate students in six fields, and 600+ other employees located in Ithaca, Geneva, and other satellite locations across New York State. The School offers the multidisciplinary undergraduate major in the Plant Sciences and is also home to the Agricultural Sciences major. School faculty also contribute to the Biology, Global Development, and Viticulture & Enology majors. Position Function This Postdoctoral Associate position will be within the Equitable Agricultural Research ( EQUAL ) Lab at Cornell. The position  requires an experienced (>2 years) researcher to develop a study on perceptions, preferences and adoption behaviors of US farmers towards a novel biotechnology product. In the first project (CROPPS,70% of time) the Postdoctoral Associate will be required to design, implement and analyze state-wide (NY and California) surveys of farmers using behavioral economics methods in the field to gauge, risk perception, willingness to pay/accept (experimental auction) or willingness to adopt, preferences and tradeoffs associated with adoption of a new biotech product. In a second project (CO-BREED, 30% of time), the Postdoctoral Associate will also lead the design, implementation and analysis of a nation-wide survey around motivations and mechanisms for US organic farmer engagement in participatory plant breeding and research, and engagement with breeding programs.. The Postdoctoral Associate will work with a graduate student for both of these projects who will be conducting related research. The position will be embedded in two projects: 1) The Center for Research on Programmable Plant Systems ( CROPPS ) project. CROPPS is a transdisciplinary team uniting faculty and trainees from the life sciences, engineering, computer science, and social sciences to pursue foundational discoveries, technological innovations, and social, ethical, and policy questions relating to Programmable Plant Systems (PPS). PPS are plants endowed with new capabilities to receive and transmit signals, enabling them to monitor and report on their environments, respond to external stimuli, and interact with other organisms and sensors. These breakthroughs create new connections between plants, people, microbes and the environment, making agriculture more adaptive, efficient, and sustainable. CROPPS unites participants from five land-grant institutions – Cornell University, University of Arizona (UA), University of Illinois Urbana Champaign (UIUC), Tuskegee University (TU), and Colorado State University (CSU) – and the Boyce Thompson Institute (BTI), with world leading capabilities and facilities for research and education. 2) CO-BREED (Collaborative Organic Breeding for Education, Engagement, and Discovery) will set up participatory plant breeding (PPB) networks for legume and vegetable crops to develop crop varieties adapted to organic farms across the US. Working with organic farmers, the team will breed African eggplant and a second emerging specialty vegetable for New American markets, specialty dry beans for the Northeast and Pacific Northwest, and winter-hardy peas for cover crop use. A national survey will examine farmer motivations for engaging in PPB. The project will train students and professionals through a graduate course, symposia, small student grants, continuing education, and Extension guides on intellectual property and seed regulations. This is an in-person position. The successful candidate will be expected to relocate within commuting distance of Ithaca, NY. Anticipated Division of Time Research method development 20% Field research 20% Data analysis 30% Writing 20% Student Support 10% Requirements Required: PhD in Behavioral Economics, Agricultural Economics or similar field Experience working with primary data such as national agricultural datasets and/or census data. Experience designing, implementing and analyzing surveys with farmers Experience in quantitative econometric research design and data analysis, with a focus on causal inference and agricultural technology adoption. Strong analytical, organizational, communication, and problem-solving skills, attention to detail, and ability to handle multiple tasks simultaneously. Ability to travel within the US for fieldwork. Driving required, must have and maintain a valid and unrestricted NY State Driver’s License and be cleared for university business. Upon hire must register with Agile/Fleet Commander . Preferred: Experience using game-based, or product concept testing behavioral methods Demonstrated experience engaging with US commodity or specialty crop grower groups Demonstrated experience working in interdisciplinary teams with biophysical scientists Experience supporting and mentoring graduate students Supervision Exercised There are no supervisory responsibilities for this position. To apply: Please apply via Academic Jobs Online ( https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33257) . Qualified candidates should submit a short cover letter, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references via the website. Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointments to academic positions require candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. Applications will be reviewed as received, continuing until a suitable applicant is identified. CALS hiring range for this title is $63,480 to $65,000. College of Agriculture and Life Sciences Life. Changing. Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League and Land-grant university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students, and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery, and engagement. Cornell’s regional and global presence includes state-wide Cornell Cooperative Extension programs and offices in all counties and boroughs, global partnerships with institutions and communities engaged in life-changing research and education, the medical college’s campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, and the Cornell Tech campus on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. With a founding principle of “any person, any study,” Cornell is an equal opportunity employer . Pay Range: $62,232.00 - $88,745.00 Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-27 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    }
